{ignore}
<p><code>{nonce}</code> answers this request's nonce: 18 random bytes, base64 - one value for
the whole request, made on first use. Put it on the scripts and styles the page itself
writes: <code>&lt;script nonce="{nonce}"&gt;</code>.</p>

<p>The engine puts the same value in the Content-Security-Policy header: <code>'nonce'</code>
(quotes included) in <code>$padCsp</code> becomes <code>'nonce-&lt;the value&gt;'</code>, so
<code>$padCsp = "default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'nonce'"</code> lets exactly the
scripts that carry it run - an injected script cannot know the value. The engine never adds
it to script tags by itself, except to the scripts it writes, such as
<a href="?tag/validator">{validator}</a>'s.</p>

<p>Write it outside an <code>&#123;ignore&#125;</code> pair: inside one it is text. The value is new on every
request, so a page carrying a nonce is never stored in the page cache, and the examples here
are shown, not run.</p>
{/ignore}
